  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThis Weekend...\n\nHigh pressure centered over New England and strong upper ridging \nwill keep dry weather in place through the weekend. Expect mostly \nclear/sunny skies outside of some occasional upper sky cover. High \ntemperatures will remain above normal in the low and mid 70s. \nMeanwhile, chilly lows still anticipated each morning in the low and \nmid 40s for most. Expect some river valley fog each morning as well, \nas has been the case all week.\n\nThis Week Into Next Weekend...\n\nSeasonably strong upper ridging and surface high pressure hold over \nthe area through this week. This will keep dry conditions in place \nwith temperatures continuing to be above normal. In fact with \nnormals falling into the low and mid 60s by late this week, actual \ntemperatures in the mid and upper 70s late week could be considered \nwell above normal for this time of year. Overnight lows will \ncontinue to be chilly given good radiational cooling with values \nmostly in the 40s to low 50s.\n\nDespite the quiet weather, a couple of concerns will be noted given \nthe prolonged dry stretch. 1.) Worsening drought conditions. No real \nsurprise here as precip deficits continue to grow. 2.) Fire weather \nas we head into late week. Haven't had a whole lot of issues yet due \nto good RH recovery at night and overall pretty light winds. However \nsteady SSW winds will take hold from Wednesday onward as high \npressure moves from eastern Canada through New England and allows \nfor a little bit tighter pressure gradient over our region. This may \ncreate some good burning opportunities, or in area that have seen \nsmall fuels get too dry, could contribute to some sporadic wildfire \nconcerns.\n\nThe first part of the weekend does look dry, however a much more \nactive pattern could arrive for the late weekend into early next \nweek time frame. Not a real consistent signal to get many details \nyet, but the general idea is that upper ridging will give way to \nsome potentially strong troughing across the western and central \nCONUS. Potential for a stronger storm system into early next week \ndoes appear possible, although a lot of details and time to work \nthrough first.",
